```

Notice that in this contract, the `PUT_RemoveContract` method performs a `Remove` operation on `arrList`, but it does not return any value. Also, the `PUT_AddContract` method returns an `Add` operation on `arrList`, but it does not affect the variables that are part of the precondition, thus the method is not complete as it should ideally cover both post-conditions and pre-conditions. 

In our wrapper API test class, we will only be able to test the `Add` and `Remove` methods under this class, due to the presence of the `arrList.Add` and `arrList.Remove` methods. The current test class does not cover the `PUT_AddContract` and `PUT_RemoveContract` methods under all possible situations, for example, when the value that is added/removed is a duplicate value, or when the list is empty or is full, etc... This means we may miss out on some potentially buggy behavior. We need to refactor this into multiple methods covering various scenarios. For example, the following method can be created:

```
public void PUT_RemoveContract([PexAssumeUnderTest] ArrayList arrList, int x)
{
	AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);
	AssumePrecondition.IsNotNull(arrList.Clone);
	AssumePrecondition.IsNotNull(arrList.Contains);
	AssumePrecondition.IsNotNull(arrList.IndexOf);
	AssumePrecondition.IsNotNull(arrList[0]);

	/* OLD VALUES */
	int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
	int Old_x = x;
	int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
	int Old_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
	bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

	arrList.Remove(x);

	/* NEW VALUES */
	int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
	int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
	int New_arrListLastIndexOfX = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);
	bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", Old_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_LastIndexOf_x", New_arrListLastIndexOfX);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
	PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
}
```
This method assert that our remove operation doesn't unintentionally corrupt the list. However, to fully test the contract, we should have an equivalent `PUT_AddContract` method for the case where we add a new item to the list. If we only have one method, our test coverage might be lacking.--------------------	    // or new array list size has been decremented by @@ one if array list contains x to begin with.
